dict(id='S17', act=1, card="The Person Behind You"),
# ---------------- ACT 2: THE PERSON BEHIND YOU ----------------
dict(id='S18', act=2, set='market', vis='Freeze at the register. Jo holds a cereal box; the price tag pops up big. Camera pushes to the tag.',
  lines=[('N', "For now, stay at the register. The store paid the fee, but that's not quite the same thing as saying the store bore the cost. So who did bear it?"),
         ('N', "Look at the price tag on the cereal you bought. The store set that price weeks ago. It has no idea how you're going to pay.")]),
dict(id='S19', act=2, set='market', vis='Back office: Gus\'s cost board: RENT, ELECTRICITY, CARD FEES ~2%. Each tag drips into the shelf prices, which tick up a few cents.',
  lines=[('N', "A card fee of two percent or so is a cost of doing business, like rent or electricity. And over time, costs like that tend to show up in prices. Not as a line on your receipt. Just a little higher for everyone.")]),
dict(id='S20', act=2, set='diner', vis='A small café counter sign: "CARD +3% / CASH DISCOUNT". Gus hangs it.',
  lines=[('N', "Some stores do split it out. They add a surcharge for paying by card, or offer a discount for cash. In the US, that's generally allowed within state laws and card network rules, and more small businesses have started doing it.")]),
dict(id='S21', act=2, set='market', vis='Behind Jo in line: Ray with cash in hand and a student with a debit card. Same cereal box, same price. Nobody hands them $2.',
  lines=[('N', "But where a store keeps one price for everyone, the fee is simply part of the price. And at that one price, who's standing behind you in line?"),
         ('N', "Someone paying cash. Someone using a debit card, which costs the store much less to accept. They pay the same price for the same cereal. Nobody hands them two dollars back."),
         ('RAY', "Where's my two bucks?")]),
dict(id='S22', act=2, set='study', vis='NBER working paper on the desk, April 2026. Coins flow along a chute from CASH & DEBIT jars to a CREDIT jar. Counter: $30 BILLION / YEAR.',
  lines=[('N', "A working paper released in April twenty twenty-six by the National Bureau of Economic Research puts a number on this. It estimates that card fees move about thirty billion dollars a year from people who pay with cash or debit to people who pay with credit.")]),
dict(id='S23', act=2, set='market', vis='Ray and the debit student look at each other; tag pops over the debit card: "PAYS A SHARE TOO".',
  lines=[('N', "That's the shape of the story you may have heard before. The rewards are paid for, in part, by the people who don't get them. And it isn't only cash. The paper finds debit card users pay for a substantial share too.")]),
dict(id='S24', act=2, set='maptable', vis='Map table of a town: an airport and a mall glow CREDIT gold; a grocery store and gas station glow CASH/DEBIT green. Low fee tags float over the big grocery chain.',
  lines=[('N', "But that thirty billion already includes a twist the usual version leaves out. People don't all shop in the same places. Travel and retail lean toward credit cards. Grocery stores and gas stations see more cash and debit."),
         ('N', "And fees aren't the same everywhere. At big grocery chains, where all kinds of shoppers overlap, the fees tend to be lower.")]),
dict(id='S25', act=2, set='maptable', vis='The $30B coin stack shrinks by a quarter but stays tall. A scale tips slightly from a modest house to a mansion.',
  lines=[('N', "Those two effects make the transfer about a quarter smaller than it would otherwise be. It's still large."),
         ('N', "And because credit cards, especially premium ones, are used more by higher-income households, part of it still flows, on balance, from lower-income households to higher-income ones. Just smaller and messier than the simple story.")]),
dict(id='S26', act=2, set='bank', vis='The Banker holds two coins up to the lamp: $1.90 and $2.00. Tag: SUSPICIOUSLY CLOSE.',
  lines=[('N', "So the person behind you is paying for part of your two dollars. Just not all of it, and not the same amount in every store."),
         ('N', "And there's something odd in our own example. On a typical purchase, the bank's slice is about a dollar ninety. Your reward was two dollars. Those numbers are suspiciously close.")]),
dict(id='S27', act=2, set='study', vis='Magnifying glass slides across the ledger. Tag: DOES THE BANK MAKE MONEY ON THIS?',
  lines=[('N', "So we've found people who help fund the rewards. We still haven't established whether the bank makes money giving them out. To find out, we have to do what the bank does, and look at the purchase side of the business on its own.")]),
dict(id='S28', act=2, card="Two Businesses, One Card"),
# ---------------- ACT 3: TWO BUSINESSES ----------------
dict(id='S29', act=3, set='lender', vis='Federal Reserve archive room at night. Three economists (Mabel and two colleagues) wheel in boxes labelled 13 BANKS, 2014-2021, MONTHLY.',
  lines=[('N', "The Federal Reserve did exactly that. In twenty twenty-two, three of its economists went through data that thirteen of the largest card banks report to the Fed, month by month, from twenty fourteen to twenty twenty-one. Not a survey. The banks' own numbers."),
         ('MABEL', "Thirteen banks. Eight years. Let's split it.")]),
dict(id='S30', act=3, set='tabletop', vis='A giant credit card on the tabletop is sawed down the middle into two halves: PAYING and LENDING. Each half grows its own little shopfront.',
  lines=[('N', "They split the credit card into its two jobs. One job is paying. You buy something now without cash. The other job is lending. You carry a balance, and interest starts to run."),
         ('N', "That's the idea at the center of this video. A credit card is two businesses sharing one piece of plastic.")]),
dict(id='S31', act=3, set='tabletop', vis='The PAYING shop: income jar (INTERCHANGE + ANNUAL FEES), expense jars (REWARDS, FRAUD, FLOAT). A clock shows the days between tap and due date.',
  lines=[('N', "The paying business earns interchange, plus any annual fees. It pays for the rewards, for fraud, and for covering your purchase until the bill is due."),
         ('N', "That last one is easy to miss. When you tap, your bank pays for your groceries long before you pay your bank. In between, it's lending you that hundred dollars for free, as long as you pay in full.")]),
dict(id='S32', act=3, set='tabletop', vis='Old balance scale: left pan $1.30 IN, right pan $1.50 OUT. The scale tips down to the red side. Water line rises: UNDERWATER.',
  lines=[('N', "So on its own, does the paying business make money? Here's what the Fed found. By the end of twenty twenty-one, after all the other costs of handling purchases, the banks were left with about a dollar thirty for every hundred dollars their customers spent."),
         ('N', "Then came the rewards. About a dollar fifty. A dollar thirty in. A dollar fifty out. On its own, the paying business was slightly underwater.")]),
dict(id='S33', act=3, set='club', vis='A racetrack of banks in jockey silks racing with giant coin-shaped rewards; a margin line on the scoreboard slides below zero. Tags: REWARDS +25% (2015-2020), -4%.',
  lines=[('N', "How does a business end up paying out more than it takes in? It hadn't always been that way. At first, the purchase side still made a little money. Then the reward race heated up."),
         ('N', "Between twenty fifteen and twenty twenty, rewards cost about a quarter more per dollar of purchases. Spending on rewards cards grew faster than spending on cards without them, and the margin on purchases slid below zero."),
         ('N', "Across the whole period, the paying side added slightly less than nothing to the bank's card profits. About minus four percent.")]),
dict(id='S34', act=3, set='hall', vis='Hall of premium metal cards growing on pedestals: 15% (2006) bar grows to 60% (2022). CFPB tag: 1.4% (2021) -> 1.6% (2024).',
  lines=[('N', "And the race didn't stop where the Fed's data does. High-reward premium cards, which charge stores more, went from about fifteen percent of credit card spending in two thousand six to sixty percent by twenty twenty-two."),
         ('N', "And the reward rate kept inching up. On rewards cards, the CFPB puts it at about one point six percent of spending in twenty twenty-four, up from one point four in twenty twenty-one.")]),
dict(id='S35', act=3, set='market', vis='Back at the big grocery chain register: a printed fee schedule unrolls: PREMIUM REWARDS AT BIG GROCER: $1.45 / $100. Next to it the $2 reward coin, and a tiny EU 30¢ coin.',
  lines=[('N', "And if your trip was at a big grocery chain, one published fee schedule puts interchange on a premium rewards card there at about a dollar forty-five per hundred dollars. Your reward was two. Compare Europe's thirty cents."),
         ('N', "On a trip like that, the fee alone wouldn't cover the reward.")]),
dict(id='S36', act=3, set='study', vis='A 2025 study page: a pie chart made of a pie: 86% slice slides back to a crowd of tiny customers.',
  lines=[('N', "Newer research fits that picture. A twenty twenty-five study estimates that about eighty-six percent of interchange goes back to customers as rewards. The economists who cite it put the lesson plainly. Lending is the main driver of bank profits.")]),
dict(id='S37', act=3, set='bank', vis='The Banker, not innocent: he turns two dials on his desk, REWARDS GENEROSITY and INTEREST RATE. Caveat tags slide in: AVERAGES, MANY BANKS, DATA ENDS 2021.',
  lines=[('N', "Careful, though. None of this means your bank loses money on you. The purchase side counts everyone's spending, including people who later borrow. And these are averages across many banks and many years. And the data end in twenty twenty-one."),
         ('N', "And the bank isn't an innocent bystander here. It chooses how generous to make the rewards. It sets the price of borrowing."),
         ('N', "What the data does show is stranger. A bank paying about a dollar fifty to earn about a dollar thirty, over and over, on purpose.")]),
dict(id='S38', act=3, card="The Line Nobody Reads"),
# ---------------- ACT 4: THE LENDING SIDE ----------------
dict(id='S39', act=4, set='living', vis='Jo on the sofa; her phone buzzes with "You\'re pre-approved!" mail piling up on the floor. The Banker\'s hand slides another envelope under the door.',
  lines=[('N', "Then why does your bank keep asking you to sign up for another card? Because the card isn't only a way to pay. It's also a loan you can take out without asking anyone.")]),
dict(id='S40', act=4, set='kitchen', vis='Calendar on the fridge: statement date -> due date shaded green GRACE PERIOD. Past the due date, an interest meter starts spinning.',
  lines=[('N', "Every card comes with a credit line. Pay the full statement balance by the due date, and on most cards, your purchases cost you no interest. That interest-free window is called a grace period."),
         ('N', "Leave part of the balance unpaid, and interest starts to run. That's the second business. And the line on your statement we haven't touched yet.")]),
dict(id='S41', act=4, set='tabletop', vis='Profit pie rebuilt from coins: LENDING 80% (giant), FEES 15% (late-fee envelopes), PAYING slightly negative (a tiny red crumb).',
  lines=[('N', "In the Fed study, it was doing almost all the heavy lifting. About eighty percent of credit card profits came from lending. Fees, mostly late fees, added roughly another fifteen percent. And the paying side, as we saw, was slightly negative.")]),
dict(id='S42', act=4, set='hall', vis='Two queues in a grand hall: TRANSACTORS (pay in full) and REVOLVERS (carry a balance). Half the crowd steps into the revolvers line. Tag: ~HALF OF ACTIVE ACCOUNTS (CFPB 2024).',
  lines=[('N', "So who uses the lending side? The industry has names for both groups. People who pay in full are transactors. People who carry a balance are revolvers. And there are more revolvers than you might expect."),
         ('N', "In twenty twenty-four, according to the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, about half of active credit card accounts carried a balance from one month to the next. Half. Not a fringe group. Half of the accounts people actually use.")]),
dict(id='S43', act=4, set='vault', vis='Vault doors open on two piles: $160 BILLION INTEREST, $31.3 BILLION FEES (more than half late fees). Then a $47.5B rewards pile is wheeled in beside them, a third the size.',
  lines=[('N', "So how much does the lending side bring in? In twenty twenty-four, cardholders were charged a hundred and sixty billion dollars in interest. On top of that came thirty-one point three billion in fees, more than half of them late fees."),
         ('N', "Careful with that hundred and sixty billion. It's interest charged, not profit. Banks have to fund the money they lend, and some of it never comes back. So you can't just subtract the rewards and call the rest profit."),
         ('N', "But you can put the two side by side. Forty-seven and a half billion in rewards. A hundred and sixty billion in interest charged, in the same year.")]),
dict(id='S44', act=4, set='bank', vis='The Banker flips Jo\'s card over: front reads 2% BACK, back reads 22% A YEAR. Big tag swap.',
  lines=[('N', "And what is that borrowing cost? At commercial banks, the average rate on card accounts that were charged interest was about twenty-two percent a year in the third quarter of twenty twenty-six."),
         ('N', "Two percent back on what you spend. Twenty-two percent a year on what you owe. Same card, different measures, and very different businesses.")]),
dict(id='S45', act=4, set='lender', vis='Economists pin bars on a corkboard: ACCOUNTS THAT NEVER REVOLVED: 40% OF PURCHASES; ALWAYS REVOLVED: <10% OF PURCHASES, >70% OF INTEREST.',
  lines=[('N', "So who's paying all that interest? In the Fed's data, accounts that carry balances pay most of the interest and most of the late fees. Put together, the researchers wrote, that suggests the people borrowing on their cards pay the majority of card banks' revenue."),
         ('N', "Accounts that hadn't carried a balance all year made about forty percent of all card purchases. Accounts that carried one every month made less than ten percent of the purchases, and paid more than seventy percent of the interest.")]),
dict(id='S46', act=4, set='tabletop', vis='Back to the split card: a fat stack of purchases lands on the PAYING half (which sinks); the interest coins pour from the borrowers into the LENDING half.',
  lines=[('N', "So a big share of the spending lands on the side of the card that runs slightly underwater. And most of the interest comes from the people who borrow the most.")]),
dict(id='S47', act=4, set='maptable', vis='Map of the country: coins stream from poorer neighbourhoods (dim) to richer ones (lit), tag $15 BILLION / YEAR (Fed + IMF). Side note: DIFFERENT METHOD FROM THE $30B.',
  lines=[('N', "So that's who pays the interest. But who ends up with the rewards? Another team of economists, from the Fed and the International Monetary Fund, looked at exactly that."),
         ('N', "They estimated that the reward system redistributes about fifteen billion dollars a year from poorer areas to richer ones. Different method, different question from the thirty billion earlier. The two don't add up.")]),
dict(id='S48', act=4, set='bank', vis='CFPB report on the desk, quote card: "maintaining credit card operations as a whole". The Banker zooms out to a giant wall of millions of cards.',
  lines=[('N', "So the picture gets clearer. Your two dollars aren't fully covered by the purchase business. The gap, a small one, is covered by the whole card."),
         ('N', "The CFPB describes the same logic in its own report. A bank pays to fund balances for everyone, it notes, but only earns interest from people who revolve. So a bank may set the markup on its interest rates with an eye on, quote, maintaining credit card operations as a whole.")]),
dict(id='S49', act=4, set='road', vis='Crane shot rising from Jo at street level up above the city: millions of tiny card lights in a grid, a portfolio. Tag: THE PORTFOLIO.',
  lines=[('N', "That's the key. The bank doesn't judge one purchase, or one customer. It judges the portfolio. You see your card from one side. A hundred dollars, two dollars back, no interest."),
         ('N', "The bank sees millions of cards over thousands of days. From up there, one customer's good month and another's bad month aren't stories. They're a mix.")]),
dict(id='S50', act=4, card="The Line Moves"),
# ---------------- ACT 5: THE MOVING LINE ----------------
dict(id='S51', act=5, set='kitchen', vis='The statement again, the blank INTEREST CHARGED line glowing. A chalk line on the floor between two teams starts to slide.',
  lines=[('N', "The two businesses live on the same card. And a customer doesn't necessarily use them in the same way forever. Go back to your statement. That blank line. Interest charged."),
         ('N', "It's easy to picture cardholders as two fixed teams. People who pay in full, and people who don't. The data says the line between them moves.")]),
dict(id='S52', act=5, set='hall', vis='Five figures on a stage: 1 in 5 ALWAYS (red), 1 in 5 NEVER (green), a quarter IN BETWEEN who flip colors month to month. Tag: IN-BETWEENERS MADE >1/3 OF PURCHASES.',
  lines=[('N', "When the Fed's researchers sorted accounts by their past twelve months, about one in five had carried a balance every single month, and about one in five never had."),
         ('N', "About a quarter sat in between. A balance in some months, none in others. And that middle group made more than a third of all the purchases. That middle group is the same card, used both ways, in the same year.")]),
dict(id='S53', act=5, set='harbour', vis='A tide gauge in a harbour: water level marked 45% (END 2021) rising to ~50% (2024).',
  lines=[('N', "And does the overall mix stay put? No. It moves with the economy, too. At the end of twenty twenty-one, when many households had paid their cards down, about forty-five percent of active accounts carried a balance. By twenty twenty-four, it was back to about half."),
         ('N', "That alone doesn't tell us who switched sides. But the CFPB notes that the balance between people who pay in full and people who carry a balance, quote, is not necessarily static, and may change over time.")]),
dict(id='S54', act=5, set='road', vis='Night road: Jo\'s little car coughs smoke and stops. Mechanic Gus hands her a bill: $1,200.',
  lines=[('N', "Here's what that can look like for one person. One month, your car needs a repair. Twelve hundred dollars."),
         ('GUS', "Twelve hundred. Card or cash?"),
         ('JO', "Card. That's what it's for.")]),
dict(id='S55', act=5, set='kitchen', vis='Kitchen table, statement close-up: PAID $200, CARRIED $1,000, and the blank line now reads INTEREST CHARGED $19. Jo\'s face falls.',
  lines=[('N', "It goes on the card, because that's one of the things a credit card is for. This time, when the bill comes, you pay two hundred and carry the rest. The next statement looks different. The blank line has a number in it."),
         ('N', "On that thousand dollars alone, a month of interest at around twenty-two percent comes to about nineteen dollars."),
         ('JO', "Huh. There it is.")]),
dict(id='S56', act=5, set='market', vis='Same supermarket, months later: Jo taps again, still gets $2 back, but now a little loan-shaped shadow follows her card.',
  lines=[('N', "For a few months, you're using the other business on the same card. Same plastic, same two percent back on groceries, and now a loan. Then you pay it off. The line goes blank again.")]),
dict(id='S57', act=5, set='living', vis='Gentle montage in the living room: a repair bill, a calendar with a payday gap, a pharmacy bag. Caption: ILLUSTRATION, NOT A PREDICTION.',
  lines=[('N', "That's an illustration, not a prediction. Plenty of people never carry a balance. Plenty do, for reasons that have nothing to do with being careless. A repair. A gap between paychecks. A medical bill. That's what the lending side is there for."),
         ('N', "The bank doesn't need any particular customer to borrow. It runs a portfolio in which some people, in some months, do.")]),
dict(id='S58', act=5, set='bank', vis='Window display of glittering card ads: TRIPLE POINTS! Jo pauses, tempted. Then a spinning coin: 30¢.',
  lines=[('N', "And rewards are one of the top reasons people choose a card in the first place. Which brings us back to those thirty cents. If the whole card pays for the rewards, what happens to them when a government caps the fee that starts it all?")]),
dict(id='S59', act=5, card="When the Toll Shrinks"),
# ---------------- ACT 6: THE CAP + ENDING ----------------
dict(id='S60', act=6, set='harbour', vis='Europe harbour town and then an Australian dock at sunrise, calendar flips to 1 OCT 2026, toll arm drops 0.8% -> 0.3%.',
  lines=[('N', "Back to Europe. The European Union has capped interchange on consumer credit cards at zero point three percent since twenty fifteen. Australia joined it this year. On October first, twenty twenty-six, its cap on everyday credit cards dropped from zero point eight percent to zero point three."),
         ('N', "On a hundred dollars, that's thirty cents for the card's bank.")]),
dict(id='S61', act=6, set='tabletop', vis='Two jars side by side: US interchange ~1.9% fills a big reward cup ~1.5%; EU/AU 30¢ fills only a thimble.',
  lines=[('N', "In the US, rewards average around one and a half percent, funded mostly by interchange of about one point nine. A thirty-cent fee leaves very little to hand back. Where that slice is small, rewards have far less to run on.")]),
dict(id='S62', act=6, set='hall', vis='A courtroom-style hall: the law caps DEBIT at big banks (stamp), CREDIT left open. Settlement scroll: -0.1 pt for 5 yrs, standard cards capped at 1.25% for 8 yrs, ~$38B. Big stamp: PENDING. Retailers object.',
  lines=[('N', "In the US, the law caps debit card fees at big banks, but not credit card interchange. A settlement with Visa and Mastercard would trim that fee by a tenth of a percentage point for five years, and cap standard cards at one point two five percent for eight."),
         ('N', "Its estimated value is about thirty-eight billion dollars. Large retailers have objected, and final approval is still pending. The reward is big where the fee is big.")]),
dict(id='S63', act=6, set='kitchen', vis='Back at the kitchen table, same lamp as the opening. The statement: Groceries $100, Cash back $2, Interest charged: blank. Camera slowly pulls back through the window to a street of lit windows; each window shows a different statement.',
  lines=[('N', "Back at your kitchen table, there's this month's statement. Groceries, a hundred dollars. Cash back, two dollars. Interest charged, blank."),
         ('N', "Somewhere else, there's a store's fee. A price tag a little higher for the person behind you. A small red line in a bank's ledger. And other statements where that line isn't blank.")]),
dict(id='S64', act=6, set='kitchen', vis='Jo flips the $2 coin; it lands. Fade to a clean end-screen plate (Stromi logo corner only) for the final 5+ s.',
  lines=[('N', "You got the two dollars back. The costs just don't all show up on your statement.")], tail=6.0),
